      "text": "To solve for \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we often convert the angle from radians to a form that is easier to handle in the unit circle. This involves finding a coterminal angle within one full rotation of the unit circle (i.e., an angle that completes one full rotation plus an additional angle).\n\n1. First, we note that angles larger than \\(2k\\pi\\) (where \\(k\\) is any integer) can be reduced by subtracting multiples of \\(2\\pi\\) (since \\(2\\pi\\) represents a full rotation around the unit circle):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}.\n   \\]\n   Hence, \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) coterminal with \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) in the unit circle.\n\n2. Looking at the reference angle:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = 3\\pi - \\frac{4\\pi}{3}.\n   \\]\n   The angle \\(3\\pi - \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\) is in the fourth quadrant since \\(-\\pi < \\frac{4\\pi}{3} < 0\\).\n\n3. The sine of our angle is then equal to the sine of the reference angle minus the difference of the angles:\n   \\[\n   \\sin\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right).\n   \\]\n   We know that \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nPutting it all together in Python using the sympy library:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the input angle in radians\nangle = 11 * sp.pi / 3\n\n# Reduce the angle to its coterminal angle within one full rotation\n# Since angle = 3π - (4π/3) where -π < 4π/3 < 0\nreduced_angle = angle - 2 * sp.pi\n\n# Calculate the sine of the reduced angle\nsine_value = sp.sin(reduced_angle)\nprint(sine_value)\n```\n```output\n-sqrt(3)/2\n```\nThe value of \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\) is \\(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nSo the final answer is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\\).",
